{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ועלו ובאו וגו' ובבית עבדיך ובעמך", :translit "ve'alu uva'u vegomer uveveite avadekha uve'amekha", :gloss "and they shall go up and come, etc., and in your servants' houses and in your people"} {:he "ובכה ובעמך ובכל עבדיך", :translit "uvekhakha uve'amekha uvekhol avadekha", :gloss "and in you and in your people and in all your servants"} {:he "ובכה", :translit "uvekhakha", :gloss "and in you"} {:he "ויאמר אל עמו", :translit "vayomer el amo", :gloss "and he said to his people"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Wordplay: ובכה read as related to בכה/נכה, meaning to strike or bang the body"} {:kind :other, :note "Reversal of order: servants precede people in Exodus 7:28 but people precedes servants in Exodus 8:13; explained by distinguishing onset vs withdrawal of plague"}], :frame {:text "A midrashic reading derives from the word ובכה in Exodus 8:13(8:9) that the frogs struck the Egyptians' bodies, reading the word as though from the root נכה or בכה, to strike or pound. Pharaoh and his people are identified as the principals in the counsel against Israel based on Exodus 1:9, 'ויאמר אל עמו'. The Ba'al HaTurim applies this to explain the reversed ordering of 'servants' and 'people' between the plague's onset (Exodus 7:28) and its withdrawal (Exodus 8:13).", :sources ["Exodus 1:9" "Exodus 7:28" "Exodus 8:13"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Wordplay: ובכה read as related to בכה/נכה, meaning to strike or bang the body", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Reversal of order: servants precede people in Exodus 7:28 but people precedes servants in Exodus 8:13; explained by distinguishing onset vs withdrawal of plague", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :exodus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:14:20.606484941Z", :model "glm-5.2"}